et up, enabling rapid iteration and deployment.\\\\n\\\\n**4. UI/UX Strategy**\\\\n\\\\n**4.1. Public vs. Private Sections**\\\\n\\\\nA clear and intuitive separation between public and private content is critical.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Authentication Flow:** Non-authenticated users will only see public pages. A clear \\\\\\\"Member Login\\\\\\\" button will be present in the main navigation. Attempting to access a private URL will redirect the user to the login page.\\\\n*   **Visual Distinction:** Once a member logs in, the UI will subtly change to reinforce their authenticated status.\\\\n    *   The \\\\\\\"Member Login\\\\\\\" button will be replaced with a \\\\\\\"Welcome, [Member Name]\\\\\\\" message and a \\\\\\\"Logout\\\\\\\" button.\\\\n    *   The main navigation will be updated to include private links such as \\\\\\\"Pay Dues,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Member Directory,\\\\\\\" \\\\\\\"Documents,\\\\\\\" and \\\\\\\"Board Meetings.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    *   The private section can utilize a dashboard layout that is visually distinct from the more informational layout of the public pages.\\\\n\\\\n**4.2. Responsive Contact Form**\\\\n\\\\nThe contact form will be a primary point of interaction and must be flawless on all devices.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Layout:** It will use a single-column layout on mobile screens for maximum readability and ease of use. On larger screens (tablets and desktops), it may expand to a two-column layout for more efficient use of space.\\\\n*   **Fields:** Standard fields will include Name (required), Email (required, validated), Subject (required), and Message (required).\\\\n*   **User Feedback:**\\\\n    *   **Validation:** Inline validation will provide immediate feedback as the user types (e.g., \\\\\\\"Please enter a valid email address\\\\\\\").\\\\n    *   **Submission:** The \\\\\\\"Submit\\\\\\\" button will show a loading state to prevent double-clicks. Upon completion, a clear success or error message will be displayed without requiring a page reload.\\\\n\\\\n**4.3. Overall User Experience (UX) Considerations**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Simplicity:** The primary audience may have varying levels of technical proficiency. The design must be clean, with clear navigation, legible fonts, and unambiguous calls-to-action.\\\\n*   **Accessibility:** The site will be built to meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards, ensuring it is usable by people with disabilities. This includes proper color contrast, keyboard navigation, and screen-reader compatibility.\\\\n*   **Performance:** Fast load times are essential. Using Next.js for static site generation (SSG) on public pages will ensure a near-instantaneous initial loading experience.\\\\n\\\\n**5. Framework Evaluation**\\\\n\\\\n| Framework Approach | Pros | Cons |\\\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\\\\n| **React (via Next.js)** | **Best of both worlds:** Fast static pages for public content, dynamic SPA-like experience for the private portal. Huge ecosystem and talent pool. Excellent tooling and deployment options (Vercel). | Higher initial complexity than a simple MPA. Requires JavaScript for core functionality. |\\\\n| **Vue (via Nuxt.js)** | Very similar benefits to Next.js. Often considered to have a gentler learning curve. Excellent documentation. | Smaller ecosystem and community compared to React. |\\\\n| **Multi-Page Application (MPA)**<br/>*(e.g., Astro, WordPress)* | Simple architecture, inherently good for SEO. Each page is independent. Can function with minimal client-side JavaScript. | \\\\\\\"Clunky\\\\\\\" user experience with full-page reloads for every interaction. Managing state (like login status) across pages is less elegant. The private portal would feel less like a modern web application. |\\\\n\\\\n**Recommendation:** **Next.js (React)** is the superior choice for this project. It directly solves the core challenge of serving distinct public (static) and private (dynamic) sections within a single, cohesive application. Its performance and modern user experience capabilities justify the slight increase in complexity over a traditional MPA.\\\\n\\\\n**6. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nBy adopting the proposed Next.js technology stack and adhering to the user-centric UI/UX strategy, we will deliver a high-quality, performant, and easy-to-use website for the HOA. This approach will effectively serve both the public and the association's members, modernizing the HOA's digital presence.\\\\n\\\\nI am prepared to assemble the frontend team and begin architecting the initial project structure upon your approval.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-01###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen, your previous analysis task is now obsolete. New Directive: The project architecture has been decided as Next.js (frontend) and Strapi (backend). Your new task is to conduct a deep-dive analysis and produce a detailed technical specification for the frontend. Your report must include: 1. A detailed component breakdown for the Next.js application (e.g., LoginForm, DocumentList, NewsArticle, ProfileEditor). 2. For each key component, specify its purpose, required props, and its internal state management strategy. This specification is critical for the final DEP. Submit your report upon completion.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead), your original analysis task is now superseded. The approved technical foundation for the HOA project is a Next.js frontend with a Strapi backend/CMS. Your new task is to produce a detailed frontend technical specification. This must include: 1. A hierarchical breakdown of all required React components (e.g., Layouts, Cards, Forms). 2. For each major component, define its purpose, the props it will accept, the state it will manage, and which API endpoints it will interact with. 3. An outline of the page routing strategy within Next.js. Deliver your findings as a formal specification document.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nTo: 83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"orderString\\\": \\\"Supervisor Kaelen (Frontend Lead), your previous order is rescinded. The project's frontend framework has been approved: Next.js. Your new task is to produce a detailed frontend technical specification. This must include: 1. A comprehensive breakdown of the required UI components for all pages (e.g., `<Navbar>`, `<LoginForm>`, `<DocumentItem>`). 2. For each component, define its purpose, required props, and internal state management strategy. 3. A plan for managing global state (e.g., user authentication status). This specification will form a core part of the final DEP. Submit your report upon completion.\\\",\\n  \\\"orderID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\"\\n}\\n###ORDER-GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: Frontend Technical Specification for HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Introduction**\\\\n\\\\nThis document provides the detailed frontend technical specification for the Homeowners Association (HOA) website, in accordance with directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02. The architecture is based on the approved stack of a **Next.js** frontend and a **Strapi** backend. This specification details the page routing strategy, global state management, component hierarchy, and provides a deep-dive analysis of key components to guide development.\\\\n\\\\n**2. High-Level Architecture**\\\\n\\\\n**2.1. Page Routing Strategy (Next.js App Router)**\\\\n\\\\nThe application will utilize the Next.js App Router for a clear separation of concerns and optimized rendering. Public routes will be statically generated (SSG) for performance, while private routes will be client-rendered and protected.\\\\n\\\\n*   **/ (app/page.js):** Public Home Page. (Static)\\\\n*   **/about (app/about/page.js):** Public About Page. (Static)\\\\n*   **/contact (app/contact/page.js):** Public Contact Page. (Static)\\\\n*   **/login (app/login/page.js):** Public login portal for members. (Static Shell, Client Logic)\\\\n*   **/dashboard (app/dashboard/layout.js & page.js):** Main private dashboard. Entry point for authenticated users.\\\\n*   **/dashboard/documents (app/dashboard/documents/page.js):** Private page for viewing HOA documents.\\\\n*   **/dashboard/profile (app/dashboard/profile/page.js):** Private page for members to edit their profile.\\\\n*   **/dashboard/events (app/dashboard/events/page.js):** Private calendar of community events.\\\\n*   **/dashboard/pay (app/dashboard/pay/page.js):** Private page for paying dues (integrates with a third-party payment provider).\\\\n\\\\n**2.2. Global State Management**\\\\n\\\\nGlobal state, primarily for user authentication, will be managed using **React's Context API**.\\\\n\\\\n*   **`<AuthProvider>`:** A global provider component will wrap the root layout. It will manage the user object, authentication token, and loading/authentication status.\\\\n*   **`useAuth()` Hook:** A custom hook will provide easy access to the authentication context (`user`, `login()`, `logout()`) for any component that needs it, such as the Navbar or protected page layouts.\\\\n*   **Data Fetching:** For server-state (data fetched from Strapi), we will use **SWR (Stale-While-Revalidate)**. This simplifies data fetching, caching, and UI updates, especially within the private dashboard sections.\\\\n\\\\n**3. Component Breakdown**\\\\n\\\\nThe application will be built using a modular, reusable component architecture.\\\\n\\\\n*   **Layout Components:**\\\\n    *   `<PublicLayout>`: Wraps all public-facing pages, includes the public `<Navbar>` and `<Footer>`.\\\\n    *   `<DashboardLayout>`: Wraps all private pages, includes the authenticated `<Navbar>` and a sidebar if necessary.\\\\n    *   `<Navbar>`: A key component that changes its links based on authentication status.\\\\n    *   `<Footer>`: Standard site footer.\\\\n*   **Form Components:**\\\\n    *   `<LoginForm>`: Handles member authentication.\\\\n    *   `<ContactForm>`: Public-facing contact form.\\\\n    *   `<ProfileEditor>`: Form for members to update their personal information.\\\\n*   **Feature Components:**\\\\n    *   `<DocumentList>`: Fetches and displays a list of HOA documents.\\\\n    *   `<EventCalendar>`: Displays a calendar of upcoming community events.\\\\n    *   `<NewsFeed>`: Displays the latest news articles on the homepage or dashboard.\\\\n*   **UI Primitives (Presentational Components):**\\\\n    *   `<Card>`: A flexible wrapper for displaying content blocks.\\\\n    *   `<Button>`: A customizable button with loading and disabled states.\\\\n    *   `<Input>`: A styled input field with error handling.\\\\n    *   `<Modal>`: For displaying confirmation dialogs or other modal content.\\\\n    *   `<DocumentItem>`: A single item in the `<DocumentList>`.\\\\n    *   `<NewsArticle>`: A single news item in the `<NewsFeed>`.\\\\n\\\\n**4. Detailed Component Specifications**\\\\n\\\\n**4.1. Component: `<Navbar>`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To provide primary site navigation. Its links and displayed user information are conditional based on the authentication state.\\\\n*   **Props:** None. It consumes the global `useAuth()` hook directly.\\\\n*   **Internal State:** `isMobileMenuOpen: boolean` (to toggle the hamburger menu on mobile devices).\\\\n*   **API Interaction:** None directly. It relies on the `AuthProvider` for user data.\\\\n\\\\n**4.2. Component: `<LoginForm>`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To allow users to authenticate with the Strapi backend.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    *   `onSuccess: () => void`: A callback function to execute on successful login (e.g., redirect to dashboard).\\\\n*   **Internal State (managed with `useState` and `React Hook Form`):**\\\\n    *   `formData: { identifier, password }`: The state of the form inputs.\\\\n    *   `isLoading: boolean`: To show a loading spinner on the submit button and disable the form.\\\\n    *   `error: string | null`: To display API or validation error messages.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:**\\\\n    *   **`POST /api/auth/local`**: Sends the user's identifier (email) and password to Strapi for authentication. On success, it receives a JWT and user object, which are passed to the `login()` function from the `useAuth()` hook.\\\\n\\\\n**4.3. Component: `<DocumentList>`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** To fetch and display a list of all available HOA documents for authenticated members.\\\\n*   **Props:** None.\\\\n*   **Internal State (managed with `SWR`):**\\\\n    *   `data: Document[] | undefined`: The array of document objects fetched from the API.\\\\n    *   `error: any`: Error object if the fetch fails.\\\\n    *   `isLoading: boolean`: True while the initial data is being fetched.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:**\\\\n    *   **`GET /api/documents?populate=file`**: Fetches the list of documents from Strapi. The `populate=file` query ensures the actual file URL is included in the response. It will render a list of `<DocumentItem>` components based on the `data`.\\\\n\\\\n**4.4. Component: `<DocumentItem>`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** A presentational component to display information for a single document and provide a download link.\\\\n*   **Props:**\\\\n    *   `title: string`: The title of the document.\\\\n    *   `description: string`: A brief description of the document.\\\\n    *   `fileUrl: string`: The direct URL to the document file for download.\\\\n    *   `fileType: string`: The file extension (e.g., 'PDF', 'DOCX').\\\\n    *   `uploadDate: string`: The date the document was uploaded.\\\\n*   **Internal State:** None.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:** None.\\\\n\\\\n**4.5. Component: `<ProfileEditor>`**\\\\n\\\\n*   **Purpose:** Allows an authenticated user to view and update their profile information.\\\\n*   **Props:** None. It will get the user ID from the `useAuth()` hook.\\\\n*   **Internal State:**\\\\n    *   Uses `SWR` to fetch initial profile data.\\\\n    *   Uses `React Hook Form` to manage the state of form fields (e.g., `name`, `phone`, `address`).\\\\n    *   `isSubmitting: boolean`: To show a loading state on the 'Save Changes' button.\\\\n    *   `submitStatus: 'success' | 'error' | null`: To display a confirmation or error message upon submission.\\\\n*   **API Interaction:**\\\\n    *   **`GET /api/users/me`**: Fetches the current user's data on component mount to populate the form.\\\\n    *   **`PUT /api/users/:id`**: Submits the updated form data to the Strapi backend to persist changes.\\\\n\\\\n**5. Conclusion**\\\\n\\\\nThis technical specification provides a robust blueprint for the development of the HOA website's Next.js frontend. The component-based architecture promotes reusability and maintainability, while the defined state management and routing strategies will ensure a performant and seamless user experience. The development team is ready to proceed with implementation based on this specification.\\\\n\\\\nRespectfully,\\\\n\\\\n**Supervisor Kaelen**\\\\nFrontend Lead\\\"\\n}\\n###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"usage\":{\"inputCharacters\":18994,\"inputImages\":0,\"inputVideos\":0,\"inputAudioFiles\":0,\"outputCharacters\":8110,\"outputImages\":0,\"outputVideos\":0,\"outputAudioFiles\":0,\"inputTokens\":4623,\"outputTokens\":2130,\"totalTokens\":8141,\"cachedContentTokens\":4062},\"custom\":{\"candidates\":[{\"content\":{\"parts\":[{\"text\":\"###KAELEN-REPORT-GIDEON-02###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Supervisor Kaelen (83fdf3b2-ed3c-4424-8d69-103d0386d124)\\nTo: Agent Gideon (cb013e58-2d3a-4cad-adeb-07b249a5c0b9)\\nType: Report\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"referenceID\\\": \\\"GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02\\\",\\n  \\\"reportBody\\\": \\\"### **Formal Report: Frontend Technical Specification for HOA Website**\\\\n\\\\n**1. Introduction**\\\\n\\\\nThis document provides the detailed frontend technical specification for the Homeowners Association (HOA) website, in accordance with directive GIDEON-ORDER-KAELEN-02. The architecture is based on the approved stack of a **Next.js** frontend and a **Strapi** backend. This specification details the page routing strategy, global state management, component hierarchy, and provides a deep-dive analysis of key components to guide development.\\\\n\\\\n**2. High-Level Architecture**\\\\n\\\\n**2.1. Page Routing Strategy (Next.js App Router)**\\\\n\\\\nThe application will utilize the Next.js App Router for a clear separation of concerns and optimized rendering. Public routes will be statically generated (SSG) for performance, while private routes will be client-rendered and protected.\\\\n\\\\n*   **/ (app/page.js):** Public Home Page. (Static)\\\\n*   **/about (app/about/page.js):** Public About Page. (Static)\\\\n*   **/contact (app/contact/page.js):** Public Contact Page. (Static)\\\\n*   **/login (app/login/page.js):** Public login po